+### This round's rulings (the supervisor's, `run/supervisor/task077-r2-rulings.md`), each to fix
+
+**Ruling 1. Every failed run of the window is read.** For every completed run of the window whose conclusion is `failure`, read its jobs, then each failed job's log. Any run of the window whose exit line reads `exit 1` is a finding. It becomes the standing finding (the newest such run's id) unless a run of the window created later than it ended `success`. The week's own kind is classified from the window's last completed run as before (green / scanner failure / no completed run). A finding found in the window, or standing from before, is reported beside that kind, not instead of it. Fault: `scan_project` (lines 690-715) reads the jobs and logs of the last completed run only (`kind == "pending_failure"`, `run = last_marker`), and `classify_window_runs` (615-642) looks at nothing but the last completed run. An earlier failed run with exit 1 is never read.
+
+**Ruling 2. A standing finding never cuts the classification short.** The week's runs are classified and read first, jobs and logs of failed runs included. Then the standing line is written beside the week's kind. `last_run_read` moves to the newest completed run of the window whenever the window completed any run, whatever the kind. Fault: `classify_window_runs` lines 618-619 return `"finding"` as soon as a finding stands and no run succeeded, before any classification, with `advance_last` False. So the week's kind is lost and `last_run_read` stays put (`scan_project` 685-689).
+
+**Ruling 3. Clearing a standing finding.** The standing finding is cleared when any run of the window, created later than the standing run, ended `success`, even if that success is not the last completed run. Where the standing run's time is unknown, compare against `last_run_read` instead. Fault: `scan_project` line 685 keeps `standing` unless the week's kind is `green`, so an earlier success of the window, followed by a later failure, never clears it. Line 618 does not compare times with the standing run at all.
+
+**Ruling 4. The alerts are read for every project with a remote.** Reads (d) and (e) run whatever the scan's kind: not configured, a disabled workflow, and a failed read of (a)-(c) included. Every section of a project with a remote has its alerts' line: the open alerts named, no alert open, alerts off, or the alerts read failed. Fault: `scan_project` returns before the alerts' reads (716-741) at lines 659-660 (the workflows read failed), 662-667 (not configured), 668-675 (disabled), 679-681 (the runs read failed), 694-696 (the jobs read failed) and 703-705 (a log read failed).
+
+**Ruling 5. A failed read names the read and the failure.** It is written with a fixed phrase naming which read failed and how, on a line like `read failed: the runs list, timed out`.
+- The reads are named: `workflows list`, `runs list`, `jobs list`, `job log`, `alerts check`, `open alerts list`.
+- The failures are named: `refused (HTTP 403)`; `refused (HTTP <code>)` for another 4xx/5xx status the reply shows; `timed out`; `gh could not start`; `gh exited non-zero`; `reply could not be parsed`.
+
+The line stands in the section beside the scan's kind where the scan was settled; a failure of (d)/(e) never replaces the scan's kind. A section whose alerts read failed is not a green section: where its scan's last completed run ended success, the section states that result (for example `scan: the last completed run ended success`, with its `last run read:` time) without the word `green` (R6's rule that `green` appears only in a green section; `tests/test_program_security_step.py::test_r097_a_failed_alerts_read_keeps_the_scans_own_result`). The remedy names the login's scope (`gh auth refresh -s <scope>`), and for (d)/(e) also the repository's Security tab. The section of a failed (a)-(c) read is never green. Its kind line says the scan could not be read, and the word `finding` stays in the section. Faults:
+- the failure returns of `scan_project` (lines 659, 680, 695, 704, 721, 730) write only kind `finding` and a generic remedy, naming neither the read nor the failure;
+- 721 and 730 (the alerts' failures) replace the scan's kind;
+- `read_failure_remedy` (645-649);
+- `_gh_paginate_json` line 547 reports every failure as `gh exited`;
+- `_gh_api_include` (519-541).
+
+**Ruling 6. Printable ASCII only.** Every value written to `security.md` is reduced to printable ASCII: project names, OWNER/REPO, times, ids, package names, advisory ids, remedies. Every character outside 0x20-0x7E (non-ASCII, ASCII control bytes and DEL alike) is written as a visible escape (`\xNN`, `\uNNNN`). `pace-report.py`'s `_ascii_name` gets the same rule for the names it prints. Faults:
